[38;5;231m⏺[39m I went with option 1, the cert-based one, so staging connections still check certificates.

  - [1m[38;5;153mclient.py[0m: both requests now pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY[39m. [38;5;153mVERIFY[39m holds the path in [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m if that variable is set;
    otherwise it's [38;5;153mTrue[39m, which means the normal certificate checks.
  - [1m[38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[0m: sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m to [38;5;153mcerts/staging-ca.pem[39m at the repo root, unless it's already set in
    your environment.
  - [1m[38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[0m: unchanged, so production still uses the system's trusted certificates.

  The only check I ran was that the edited files parse. I haven't run either script against a server.

  [1mYou need to do one thing:[0m save the staging certificate as [38;5;153mcerts/staging-ca.pem[39m, or point [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m at wherever
  it lives. You can grab it with [38;5;153mopenssl s_client -connect reports.staging.example:443 -showcerts </dev/null | openssl [39m
  [38;5;153mx509 > certs/staging-ca.pem[39m. Until that file exists, [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m will fail with a "file not found" error.

  Nothing is committed yet.

